[Music]  you are the CEO of a large multinational  company your company has a hundred and  seventy billion dollars in revenue it  serves 1.2 billion customers and it  employs almost a million people but it's  not all good news for you  in the past few decades your company has  lost revenue and market share at pretty  alarming rates your home markets of  North America and Europe are the ones  that have been hit the hardest off that  only by growth in emerging markets like  Latin America and Africa but that's not  gonna be good enough you're the CEO of  this business what are you gonna do my  name is Sara Joyce and the organization  that I just described to you is the  Catholic Church the Catholic Church is a  company that I've always been interested  in I grew up in a Catholic household I  went to a Catholic school I even worked  as an altar server helping the priest  with mass on Sundays I did my grade 10  career speech about how women should be  priests thinking that I was going to  become one then my life went in a bit of  a different direction I became a  management consultant but it was only in  working and consulting for a few years  in marketing strategy that I became  interested in the church in a new way I  was helping clients with declining  revenue and declining market share  problems and I started wondering what if  I took the same common approach that I  was using on my clients and applied it  in an uncommon place the Catholic Church  what if I was the CEO of the church what  would I do but I get it the Catholic  Church is my thing it definitely might  not be yours on one hand you might not  be religious you might not be Catholic  you're wondering why you should care  about the church I get it on the other  hand maybe a really religious really  Catholic you think the church is sacred  not something to be turned around or  fixed I get that too but the beauty of  this exercise to me  is that it actually doesn't matter where  stand on the issue it's not really about  religion at all rather it's about taking  something that has typically not been  thought of with the consumer marketing  lens and just seeing what happens if we  start to think of it this way and so I  invite you to suspend disbelief and  indulge with me in this exercise of you  and I as Pope and CEO and of course it's  been a bit of a bumpy ride in getting to  the top of the church when I first  started thinking about this topic back  in November Pope Benedict was firmly in  place when I first delivered it in March  there was no Pope we had the first  resignation of the Pope in over 700  years and as I stand here today we have  Pope Francis the first pope from an  emerging market and hopefully signal of  positive change that is to come there  was a lot of really great press at the  time when this was all happening my  personal favorite from CBS News  look would that be our inspiration for  today so if we were the CEOs of the  church the first thing that we would do  is define the problem and in this case  define the problem as greater net losses  of existing customers in traditional  markets than any other major faith and  while that may sound like an unnecessary  mouthful there's a reason why I say it  like that this is a global complex  problem like any good CEO we're gonna  focus down and tackle it one issue at a  time starting here in the US it's our  home market it also happens to be the  one we have the best data on and so step  two we scan the industry and we say how  are our competitors doing  who's winning in this market and why and  when we grant grab data on religious  affiliation in the u.s. over the past  few decades we can see that the Catholic  affiliation has been relatively flat  hovering around 25 percent of the  population well that might not seem like  enough of cause for change what this  line hides is how the population base  has changed within that we've lost a lot  of existing customers and we're  benefiting here from immigration from  Latin America primarily from Mexico so  what about the Protestants when we grab  the data on them we see that they  actually have  worse than we do they were at 60% of the  population a few decades ago and they're  just about at 50 today so then what  about this one any ideas to the one  religion that's been eating our lunch I  think I heard it in there the religion  that's been winning is those who are  unaffiliated with religion or the nuns  as I will call them these nuns they they  may have been raised in in religion but  they've become unaffiliated with what  they were raised in without rejoining  with another faith and the next logical  question is who are these people and it  turns out when we ask ourselves this we  need to look no further than this room  this is the millennial generation 18 to  29 we have more choice more freedom  expression we don't need to do what our  parents did anymore and so as CEOs of  the church doesn't this seemed like a  logical intervention points to re-engage  with our millennial Catholics before  they become nuns and so we define our  target customer as the Millennial  Catholics 18 to 29 years old who were  raised Catholic but are currently not as  engaged as they used to be or as we  would like them to be and if you're with  me so far  then here comes the fun part what are we  gonna do about it I like to think about  customer engagement in three ways we  need to do something to get them into  the door we then need to do something  with them while they're here and we need  to do something that keeps them coming  back so let's go through each of these  to get them in the door this is the  Millennials we're talking about you  might think to turn to social media well  I find it extremely fascinating that the  Pope has a Twitter account with 2.4  million followers that celebrities and  athletes who are the key influencers for  this demographic are constantly tweeting  Thanksgiving thanks and praise and  appreciation to God even Jesus has his  own Twitter account  Twitter actually did a study last year  and found that some of the most popular  tweets in terms of the number of tweets  and retweets were those that had an  inspiring message about God talk about  meeting your customers where they are  and if social media is not enough maybe  another idea to get them in could be  direct outreach at some of you in the  room know there's a very famous target  campaign where they use the data that  they have to predict when someone might  be pregnant and then they sent targeted  mailings and coupons to those expectant  mothers on baby products well what if we  use the data that we had on our  demographic to predict when they might  be going through major life events when  they graduate college moved to a new  city start a new job just like the  expectant mothers were more receptive to  target in those moments wouldn't our  demographic be more receptive to us so  we've got them in the door now what are  we gonna do with them and I actually  don't think there's much you can do with  the mess the sacraments the homily or  the sacraments the the traditions  everything needs to remain the same the  church isn't gonna go for budging on  much of that but I do think there's an  opportunity to innovate the homily for  those of you who don't know the homily  is the five to 10 sometimes 15 20 30  minutes that the priest has to connect  the fundamental teachings of the church  to today's modern world some priests are  really good at this today the problem is  it's inconsistent I can't help but think  back to my family we would pile into the  minivan and we would drive for 25  minutes past our local parish to this  one particular parish to see this one  particular priest father Joe father Joe  was 6 feet tall he had long dark hair  and big booming voice he was funny and  charismatic when he spoke he walked  right up into the pews and interacted  with his audience he made you laugh he  made you cry  he made you laugh because you were  crying and that was kind of weird but  when you left you left feeling inspired  and uplifted and feeling everything that  I would want my congregation to feel if  I were the CEO so how do we hold all  priests up to the Father joe standard  I've come to realize that we can't  priests are called to serve they're not  recruited for their public speaking  abilities but there might be an  opportunity to help them out what about  hiring professional speakers to come in  every once in a while  what about hiring speech writers and  communication coaches and damn it when  we find a message that works  why not videotape it so we can share it  across the network don't make me drive  to father Joe bring father Joe and me  and so that brings me to my final point  how are we gonna keep them a CEOs of the  church we don't want our relationship  with our congregation to end when math  ends we want to give them something to  take with them into the week luckily  today we have mobile phones something  that is always with our target  demographic so what if we used a mobile  app to allow us to share content with  them allow them to share content with  one another we could have a prayer wall  we could create invites track rides  attendance we could even accept online  donations imagine there are apps like  this that exist  it's estimated that 10,000 parishes are  already using them what is the Catholic  Church waiting for why not become the  next one so hopefully by now you have  seen that an uncommon approach or a  common approach rather can work in even  the most uncommon place if I were the  CEO of the church I would go after the  millennial generation I would use social  media and direct outreach to bring them  in  I'd innovate the homily for them once  they're there and I would use a mobile  app to keep them coming back my next  steps are pretty clear I'm gonna try to  get this onto Pope Francis's desk but  what about all of you I haven't  forgotten about you what problem are you  facing what's going on in your industry  who are your customers and how are you  gonna optimize the experience for them  you are the CEO thank you  you  [Music]

